<article class="env-remark" id="00X7">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00X7">Remark <span data-tag="00X7">3.1.7.20</span> <span class="named">(Coproducts of Weak Homotopy Equivalences)</span>.</a> Let $\{  f(i): X(i) \rightarrow Y(i) \} _{i \in I}$ be a collection of weak homotopy equivalences of simplicial sets indexed by a set $I$. For every Kan complex $Z$, we have a commutative diagram of Kan complexes </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \operatorname{Fun}( \coprod _{i \in I} Y(i), Z) \ar [r] \ar [d]^{\sim } &amp;  \operatorname{Fun}( \coprod _{i \in I} X(i), Z) \ar [d]^{\sim } \\ \prod _{i \in I} \operatorname{Fun}( Y(i), Z) \ar [r] &amp;  \prod _{i \in I} \operatorname{Fun}(X(i), Z), } \]
</div>
<p> where the vertical maps are isomorphisms. Passing to the connected components (and using the fact that the functor $Q \mapsto \pi _0(Q)$ preserves products when restricted to Kan complexes; see Corollary <a data-tag="00HD" href="/tag/00HD">1.2.5.11</a>), we deduce that the map $\pi _0( \operatorname{Fun}( \coprod _{i \in I} Y(i), Z) ) \rightarrow \pi _0( \operatorname{Fun}(\coprod _{i \in I} X(i), Z) )$ is bijective. Allowing $Z$ to vary, we conclude that the induced map $\coprod _{i \in I} X(i) \rightarrow \coprod _{i \in I} Y(i)$ is also a weak homotopy equivalence. </p>
</article>
